> The PANACEA project aims to tackle the complex and interrelated challenges of climate risks and adaptation by combining the strengths of multiple methodologies. Current approaches to understanding climate impacts are often siloed—notably, process-based models, econometric techniques, and macroeconomic frameworks each have long, independent histories. This has led to gaps in our understanding of key risks, particularly around the potential and limits of adaptation. PANACEA will provide actionable information on how adaptation investments can achieve societal goals.
> 
> PANACEA will use innovative machine learning and Bayesian techniques to bridge these disparate methodologies, focusing on four critical climate risks. The project will develop new insights on losses from flooding, biodiversity loss, perennial crop damages, and macroeconomic growth. In each case, key dimensions of adaptation will be explored: respectively looking quantitatively at the role of land use change, protected areas, agricultural inputs, and supply chains. These four risk areas and adaptation actions are powerful case studies for a broader interdisciplinary improvement in our climate risks. Broadly, PANACEA emphasizes how investments and risks interact across various sectors and scales and their consequences for vulnerable communities.
> 
> In particular, the project aims use these new outcomes to support decision-making in low and lower-middle income countries, where the evidence base can be greatly improved by integrating across disciplines. Utilsing co-design workshops, PANACEA will develop interactive tools for Nigeria and Brazil, incorporating targeted scenarios and information on the local consequences of development, adaptation, and mitigation action. These tools will allow decision-makers to explore outcomes and design policy actions for both the core PANACEA sectors and the broader evidence base for risks and adaptation.
