> The PANACEA project aims to address the complex, interconnected challenges of climate risks and adaptation by harnessing the strengths of multiple methodologies. Traditional approaches to understanding climate impacts often operate in silos; process-based models, econometric techniques, and macroeconomic frameworks each have distinct, independent histories. This separation has led to gaps in our understanding of crucial risks, particularly concerning the potential and limitations of adaptation. PANACEA will provide actionable information on how adaptation investments can achieve societal goals.
> 
> Using innovative machine learning and Bayesian techniques, PANACEA will bridge these disparate methodologies while focusing on four critical climate risks. The project will offer new insights into losses from flooding, biodiversity loss, perennial crop damages, and macroeconomic growth. For each risk, key dimensions of adaptation will be explored; specifically, examining the roles of land use change, protected areas, agricultural inputs, and supply chains. These four risk areas and adaptation actions serve as powerful case studies for a broader interdisciplinary enhancement of our understanding of climate risks. Overall, PANACEA highlights the interaction between investments and risks across sectors and scales, with significant consequences for vulnerable communities.
> 
> The project particularly seeks to utilise these new outcomes to support decision-making in low and lower-middle-income countries, where the evidence base can be significantly improved by integrating across disciplines. Through co-design workshops, PANACEA will develop interactive tools for Nigeria and Brazil, incorporating targeted scenarios and information on the local consequences of development, adaptation, and mitigation actions. These tools will enable decision-makers to explore outcomes and design policy actions for both the core PANACEA sectors and the broader evidence base concerning risks and adaptation.
